Drawing on a comparative qualitative study of two eco-industrial parks in Nanjing and Jiangyin, China, this paper examines how public authorities and firms use existing production records and environmental monitoring data as shared evidence. It explains how digital verification helps regulators maintain oversight while giving firms room to adjust their operations, creating conditions for sustaining circular economy practices over time.

Oral presentation in the **Policy Levers for Circular Economy** session at EcoBalance 2026 in Nagoya, Japan.
